[Music]  so  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  hello my name is cecil slipovic and i'm  musician in residence at yale  university's  fortune of video archive for holocaust  testimonies  this video series is designed as a  companion to our album  where is our homeland which is the first  volume in the archives ongoing effort  to present songs and poems performed by  survivors in testimonies given to the  fortune of archive  over the last 40 years you can learn  more about the project on the archives  website  songs from testimonies.org  where is our homeland songs from  testimonies volume 1  was recorded in 2019 by the cecil  slupovich ensemble  featuring renowned singer sasha luria  craig udelman on the violin joshua camp  on the accordion  keyboards and guitar dmitry ischenko on  the base  and myself on woodwinds the songs in  this collection differ vastly in terms  of style  social and historical background  language and many other aspects  but collectively they create a  multi-dimensional picture  of jewish life before and during world  war  ii in this episode we are going to talk  about  and listen to another song from lubov  case testimony  tucci not buddhism the clouds gather  over buddhist gym  it is noteworthy that the entire fortune  of video archive  music project started in 2018  with lubov's testimony with the cecil  slipovich ensemble featuring the  wonderful and world-renowned singer  sasha luria  we have recorded two albums 26 songs  in total and i'm preparing two other  volumes to be recorded  with really fascinating and unique  repertoire touching buddhism  is a retexting and melodic  adaptation of pavel armand's music and  lyrics for the song  tuchi not gorodham the clouds gathered  over the city  performed by the popular soviet actor  and singer  mark bernese in the sergey youth  cabbages 1938  film celebric sorghum the man  with a gun the song quickly gained  popularity  armand's text conveys the heartbreak of  a young man  who is going to the war and is bidding  farewell  to the young woman he loves the labor  camp version  shared by lubov depicts the horrific  realities of a slave labor  hunger cold lack of basic living  facilities  and the cruelty of the ukrainian guard  step  on the song has a time and feel of a  waltz  one of the most popular genres in the  1930s  soviet urban culture  of  is  [Applause]  [Music]  my  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  is  [Music]  oh  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  m  [Music]  your wife's testimony and songs offer  important insight into some lesser-known  aspects of the history of the holocaust  while teeming concentration camps gas  chambers  and industrial crematoria still  dominate popular imagery of the  holocaust  debuff's testimony is more  representative of the experience of  soviet jews  who by and large were not deported to  the killing centers  in german-occupied poland instead in the  small towns and cities of ukraine  belarus the baltic states and western  russia  many able-bodied jewish men young  children  and elderly fell victim to mass  shootings during the early months of the  german occupation  surviving adults were condemned to force  labor in the ghettos or small labor  camps  living in constant fear of ongoing mass  shootings  dubois testimony highlights the  experience of jews  in these small virtually unknown ghettos  and camps  where grueling labor and appalling  living conditions led to high rates of  injury malnutrition and disease  buddhist show was the third camp in a  series of labor camps  where german overseers and local politsi  transported lubuf and her fellow  prisoners  as slave rape slave laborers for the  transit highway 4 project  between may 1942 and august 1943  while these jewish laborers were in  theory incredibly valuable for the  german  war effort the nazi regime allowed  so-called  accidental deaths to claim many lives  and the local gendarmerie added to the  death toll with punitive shootings  and camp liquidations of the four songs  that lubov k  shares in her testimony tuccinat  buddhism  offers the most comprehensive summation  of day-to-day life  in the small improvised labor camps  along the transit highway 4.  it recounts the frigid winter conditions  in the  unheated clubhouse where prisoners were  housed  it describes back-breaking labor they  performed  on the transit highway and their efforts  to supplement the meager food rations  with food from the local villagers  it also references the threat of the  cruel guard stephan  who meted out punishment for running off  to the village  despite the song's somber lyrics  takes visible joy in performing it for  the interviewers during her testimony  as they composed this song in the camps  dubois and her fellow prisoners  borrowed the lilting melody of a popular  soviet song  puccinad gordon from the 1938 movie  the man with a gun the fact that the  original performer of the song  mark bernese was a ukrainian jew  was no doubt a point of great pride for  these prisoners  and the melody a welcome reminder of  their lives before the war  for these reasons perhaps the song ends  with a confident assertion  that the tide of the war will turn  victory  will come and joy will be possible  once again  [Music]  you

